# Copy this file to .env for local Docker Compose runs:
#
#     cp .env.example .env          # macOS / Linux / Git Bash
#     Copy-Item .env.example .env   # Windows PowerShell
#
# Docker Compose reads .env automatically from the directory containing
# docker-compose.yml. The real .env is gitignored and must never be committed.
# Every value here is a non-secret placeholder with a working default, so the
# file is safe to commit.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redis connection used by the app container
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inside Compose the app reaches Redis by its service name, "redis".
# Point this at an external host (e.g. host.docker.internal) to use your own.
SPRING_DATA_REDIS_HOST=redis
SPRING_DATA_REDIS_PORT=6379

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production / hosted deployment (Render)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are NOT read from this file in production. They are configured in the
# Render Dashboard and are listed here only to document the names in use.
#
#   SPRING_DATA_REDIS_URL
#       Private-network connection string for Render Key Value, of the form
#       redis://red-xxxxxxxxxxxx:6379. Wired automatically in render.yaml via
#       `fromService: property: connectionString`. When set, Spring Boot ignores
#       SPRING_DATA_REDIS_HOST / _PORT above.
#
#   PORT
#       Injected by Render (default 10000). application.yml reads ${PORT:8080},
#       so the app binds the platform-assigned port automatically.
#
#   MANAGEMENT_ENDPOINTS_WEB_EXPOSURE_INCLUDE=health,info
#   MANAGEMENT_ENDPOINT_HEALTH_SHOW_DETAILS=never
#       Keeps Redis version, heap and disk internals off the public health URL.
#       Locally these are left unset, so /actuator/health keeps showing details.
